From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: edk2-cix <edk2-cix@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 23:48:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Silicon/CIX/Sky1: GlobalWatchdogDxe: raise the boot watchdog
 to 90s

The global watchdog covers everything from DXE entry to ReadyToBoot and
its expiry is an unconditional SoC reset.  20s could trip on a slow but
progressing cold boot; a hung boot still never reaches ReadyToBoot, so
the longer period keeps the hang recovery.

From Neol00/edk2-cix-unlocked V2.4.
---
 .../CIX/Sky1/Drivers/GenericWatchdogDxe/GlobalWatchdogDxe.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Silicon/CIX/Sky1/Drivers/GenericWatchdogDxe/GlobalWatchdogDxe.c b/Silicon/CIX/Sky1/Drivers/GenericWatchdogDxe/GlobalWatchdogDxe.c
index 7627ae1f..bbac50e4 100644
--- a/Silicon/CIX/Sky1/Drivers/GenericWatchdogDxe/GlobalWatchdogDxe.c
+++ b/Silicon/CIX/Sky1/Drivers/GenericWatchdogDxe/GlobalWatchdogDxe.c
@@ -14,7 +14,11 @@
 #include <Library/CixPostCodeLib.h>
 #include <Protocol/WatchdogTimer.h>
 
-#define GLOBAL_WDT_DEFAULT_TIME 20 //seconds
+// The watchdog is armed at driver entry and only disarmed at ReadyToBoot, so
+// one period has to cover console connection and device enumeration.  20s
+// could expire on a slow but healthy cold boot (USB hubs, several NVMe
+// drives, network boot attempts) and reset the board.
+#define GLOBAL_WDT_DEFAULT_TIME 90 //seconds
 
 VOID
 EFIAPI
